{
  "title": "My Voice, But Not My Soul: What Happened When I Cloned Myself",
  "slug": "my-voice-but-not-my-soul-what-happened-when-i-cloned-myself",
  "content": "I stared at my studio monitors, listening to a sentence I had never spoken in my life.\nThe cadence was mine, the subtle raspy break on the letter \"k\" was mine, and even that weird habit I have of pitching up at the end of a thought was right there in the waveform.\nIt was equal parts thrilling and deeply unsettling.\n\n## The Day I Uploaded My Vocal DNA\n\nI decided to test voice cloning six months ago during a brutal deadline crunch for a client's video series.\nI fed twenty-seven minutes of dry, clean audio into an AI platform, using a Shure SM7B mic plugged into a Sound Devices MixPre-3 recorder.\nThe audio had no background noise, no room reverb, and zero equalization or compression",
